{
  "_comment": [
    "Every environment variable this repository reads or supplies, classified by WHO",
    "SUPPLIES THE VALUE and WHO MAY READ IT. Derived and checked by",
    "`.ci/scripts/quality/check_env_manifest.py`; read that module's docstring before",
    "editing this file, because every rule below is enforced there and none of it is",
    "advisory.",
    "",
    "THERE IS NO COUNT IN THIS FILE, ANYWHERE, ON PURPOSE. The plan box that asked for",
    "it quoted 1,014 names; the box's own design note re-measured 745, then 721, then",
    "777, depending on whether one developer's `.env.pre-rename.bak` happened to be on",
    "disk. Every number the gate prints is derived on the run that prints it. If you",
    "are about to write a total into a comment here, do not: it will be wrong within",
    "the week and it will be believed.",
    "",
    "HOW TO ADD A NAME. The gate names it as UNCLASSIFIED and tells you the seven live",
    "shards. Put it in exactly ONE, in sorted order. There is deliberately no",
    "`--write-baseline`: a blanket reseed is how a fresh violation gets absorbed",
    "alongside a legitimate drain, and this file is small enough to edit by hand.",
    "",
    "HOW TO REMOVE ONE. Do not, unless the tree genuinely stopped reading it -- in",
    "which case the gate has ALREADY told you, as STALE. A live-shard entry that no",
    "reader finds is a red, not a tidy-up: that is the arm that keeps the manifest",
    "shrinking rather than accumulating names nobody uses."
  ],
  "shard_definitions": {
    "secret": "Supplied by the Bitwarden vault (.ci/config/bws-secret-map.json) or by a GitHub Actions secret, and readable only by the job or runtime that needs it. VAULT MEMBERSHIP DECIDES, not the shape of the name: ACCOUNT_ED25519_PUBLIC_KEY and STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY are public values and still belong here, because the question this shard answers is who supplies the value, and the answer for both is the vault. No value of any secret is recorded anywhere in this file or read by the gate; names only.",
    "ci-runner": "Supplied by the GitHub Actions runner (GITHUB_*, RUNNER_*, CI) or by this repository's own CI orchestration: a workflow `env:` block, a job output (RESULT_*), a dispatch input, the watchdog, the autopilot. Read only inside CI; setting one on a developer machine does nothing.",
    "gate-seam": "Supplied by a gate's own control or test so the gate can be pointed at a FIXTURE instead of the real tree, or so a threshold can be moved for one run. Read by exactly one gate. These are the names most often mistaken for configuration, and REDIACC_CI_ROOT is the canonical one -- `.ci/rediacc_ci/paths.py` declares it as the SINGLE such override for the whole package, replacing eight per-gate spellings.",
    "toolchain": "A version pin, a checksum, or the path to a tool binary. Supplied by `.devcontainer/toolchain.env` or by whatever provisioned the host. This shard is what `check:ci-toolchain-pins` and the devcontainer image agree about.",
    "machine-local": "Supplied by the developer's operating system, shell or session. This repository only ever READS these; nothing here sets them, and nothing here may require them to be set.",
    "product-runtime": "Read by SHIPPED code at run time -- the rdc CLI on a user's machine, the account worker, the marketing site. A change to one of these is a change to the product's interface, and `docs/environment-variables.md` is the user-facing reference for the CLI subset.",
    "harness": "Supplied by a local test, development or agent harness: the e2e suite, the tutorial recorder, the breakpoint lab, the VM lab, the Claude hooks. Not shipped, not CI-supplied, not a gate seam.",
    "tombstone": "Retired. The name must not appear in the derived corpus at all. Every entry has a replacement in `docs/environment-variables.md`; the gate fails if one comes back. A read that exists only to PROVE the name is dead is registered under `tombstone_proof_sites` by exact (path, name) pair."
  },
  "tombstone_proof_sites": {
    "packages/cli/src/__tests__/env-tombstones.test.ts": [
      "REDIACC_API_TOKEN",
      "REDIACC_REGION",
      "REDIACC_TEAM",
      "X25519_PUBLIC_KEY"
    ],
    "packages/cli/src/services/__tests__/account-client.test.ts": [
      "X25519_PUBLIC_KEY"
    ]
  },
  "_tombstone_proof_sites_comment": [
    "A file whose job is to NAME dead variables necessarily mentions them, and left in",
    "the corpus it resurrects them into `sources` -- breaking the tombstone clause by",
    "existing. The suppression is per (path, NAME) PAIR and never per file, and it is",
    "liveness-checked in both directions: the pair must still be found by a reader at",
    "that path (a dangling entry fails, so the list can only shrink), and the name must",
    "be in the tombstone shard (so the mechanism cannot hide a live variable). Both are",
    "printed on every run.",
    "The second entry is account-client.test.ts:164, which SETS X25519_PUBLIC_KEY and",
    "asserts the config value wins anyway. That is a proof of death, not a read."
  ],
  "collisions": [
    {
      "name": "DEBUG",
      "dead_in": "the rdc CLI",
      "authority": "docs/environment-variables.md",
      "live_in": "packages/e2e-tests/.env.example",
      "shard": "harness",
      "why": "DEBUG is a CLI tombstone (docs/environment-variables.md:107 replaces it with REDIACC_DEBUG scopes) and, separately, the `debug` npm package's own variable, which the e2e suite still sets. Two variables that share a spelling, so a repo-wide ban would be false and it is NOT in the tombstone shard. THE AUTHORITY IS THE DOC AND NOT THE TEST, and that is a finding rather than a preference: packages/cli/src/__tests__/env-tombstones.test.ts bans fourteen retired names and DEBUG is not one of them, so nothing in code enforces the documented deletion. This entry pointed at the test until a plant on the real tree showed the check passing for the wrong reason -- the match was a plain substring and REDIACC_DAEMON_DEBUG contains DEBUG. Both the matcher and the claim were wrong; both are fixed here."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "RDC_BENCH": "DOCUMENTED DEAD AND STILL READ IN BASH, found 2026-09-09 while building this file. docs/environment-variables.md lists it as deleted, CLAUDE.md and docs/agent-reference/local-env.md both say 'there is no RDC_BENCH', and .ci/scripts/test/test-rdc-sh-env.sh:81 enforces its absence from the wrapper -- but .ci/lib/local-common.sh:810 and .ci/scripts/infra/build-renet.sh:70 both branch on ${RDC_BENCH:-0}, and scripts/dev/deploy-bench.sh:286-287 PRINTS advice telling the operator to run `RDC_BENCH=1 ./rdc.sh ...`, which the wrapper no longer honours. This gate cannot see it: bash ${VAR} is outside the corpus by design. Left in the tombstone shard because that is the documented ruling, with the contradiction recorded here rather than resolved by quietly reclassifying it.",
    "REDIACC_ENVIRONMENT": "Dead as a name the CLI READS. It still travels as a REMOTE renet environment value, built as a string literal in services/executor/local-executor.ts, which is none of the five source kinds and so does not collide.",
    "REDIACC_CI_ROOT": "The single fixture-pointing override for the whole rediacc_ci package, declared once at .ci/rediacc_ci/paths.py:70 as ROOT_ENV. It is in this manifest only because the os-environ reader parses instead of grepping: `os.environ.get(ROOT_ENV)` is invisible to a regex over the call site, and 54 other gate seams were invisible with it.",
    "X": "A PLACEHOLDER, not a variable. `process.env.X` appears inside a fixture string in scripts/gates/check-gate-cwd-independence.ts:169, which builds a synthetic gate to test its own matcher. It is classified gate-seam because that is literally what it is: a seam in a gate's own fixture. The Python reader no longer produces this class of entry (it parses), but no TypeScript parser is available to a Python gate.",
    "SOME_ROOT": "The same case as X: a placeholder in the fixtures of scripts/gates/check-gate-cwd-independence.ts and in the prose of scripts/lib/repo-root.ts:15.",
    "STATE_isPost": "GitHub Actions' own state channel, written by @actions/core in .github/actions/profiler/index.js. The lowercase tail is theirs, not a typo, and it is why the yaml-key matcher permits a trailing lowercase character."
  },
